\section{Extended Limitations}
\label{app:limitations-extended}

Beyond the limitations discussed in the main text, several additional constraints bound our conclusions. Seven of our eleven model-domain conditions have $n \leq 60$, providing insufficient statistical power for confirmatory claims; power analysis shows that the minimum detectable effect exceeds 5\,pp for these conditions (Table~\ref{tab:power}), so they serve as regime characterization rather than definitive tests. Our extraction prompts use a single zero-shot template per domain; alternative prompting strategies (few-shot exemplars, chain-of-thought extraction, or iterative refinement) might yield higher-quality constraints, though the oracle gap suggests the bottleneck is structural rather than prompt-specific. The constraint quality analysis (91.8\% discriminative rate, 39.2\% source-trace minority weight) covers Qwen2.5-14B on FOLIO + ProofWriter ($N = 804$); other model-domain combinations may exhibit different characteristics. Constraint extraction uses $T_{\text{ext}} = 0.3$ while trace sampling uses $T = 0.7$; alternative extraction temperatures might yield different results. Although we prompt for first-order logic formalizations, 99.0\% of extracted constraints are propositional at the Z3 level, with only 1.0\% using true quantifiers ($\forall$/$\exists$); whether models capable of genuine quantified reasoning would yield higher discriminative rates is untested. Deduplication uses syntactic equivalence (global average dedup ratio: 46.0\%), so semantically equivalent constraints with different surface forms receive separate weights; Z3-based semantic deduplication removes an additional ${\sim}$10\% of constraints and yields a small but significant improvement over no deduplication ($p = 0.013$), suggesting room for better dedup strategies. The GSM8K pilot ($n = 30$) illustrates the constraint-absent regime and should not be interpreted as confirmatory evidence.

\section{Cross-Architecture Proof-of-Concept}
\label{sec:solutions}
\label{sec:cross-arch}

\paragraph{Method.}
We test this with DeBERTa-large \citep{he2021deberta} (406M, encoder-only), fine-tuned on MultiNLI \citep{williams2018mnli}, classifying (premise, conclusion) pairs into \textsc{entailment}, \textsc{contradiction}, or \textsc{neutral}. In combo mode, the NLI verdict is integrated with SC's $K$ votes via weight $w$: each NLI-supporting vote counts $w$ times. We evaluate on Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$) and ProofWriter depth-5 ($n = 600$), because NLI classification requires (premise, conclusion) pairs that these datasets naturally provide; multiple-choice datasets (LogiQA) lack this structure.

\paragraph{Main result.}
DeBERTa NLI alone achieves 49.51\% on FOLIO (below SC's 55.88\%) and 55.00\% on ProofWriter (above SC's 39.33\%). On FOLIO ($n = 204$), combo mode yields $+1.96$\,pp at $w{=}1$ and $+2.94$\,pp at $w{=}3$ (both n.s.). On ProofWriter ($n = 600$), $w{=}3$ achieves $+3.50$\,pp ($p = 0.013$) and $w{=}5$ reaches $+10.67$\,pp ($p < 0.001$)---while same-model SICA \emph{degrades} ProofWriter ($-2.83$\,pp, $p = 0.014$; Table~\ref{tab:main_results}), illustrating that corrective signal must originate outside the model's distribution (\S\ref{sec:mirage}). We report $w{=}3$ as primary; higher weights risk overfitting. After Benjamini--Hochberg correction ($m{=}6$), ProofWriter $w{=}3$ remains significant (adjusted $p = 0.038$) and $w{=}5$ ($p < 0.001$); FOLIO comparisons are non-significant. Per-label caveat: DeBERTa over-predicts \textsc{False} on ProofWriter (67.8\%) and performs poorly on \textsc{Unknown} (3.5\% accuracy), as NLI's ``neutral'' fails to capture logical unknowability.

\paragraph{Why it works.}
The 406M encoder-only DeBERTa and 7B decoder-only Mistral share no architecture, training data, or inference mechanism, satisfying the independence condition that same-model sampling cannot provide (\S\ref{sec:mirage}). This produces per-label complementarity: NLI excels on \textsc{False} problems (71.43\% vs.\ SC's 38.10\%) where the LLM is weakest, while SC dominates \textsc{Unknown} (68.12\% vs.\ 18.84\%). All three NLI architectures tested---DeBERTa, RoBERTa, and BART (all MNLI-trained)---produce significant improvements on ProofWriter at $w \geq 3$ (Appendix~\ref{app:multi-nli}), confirming the effect is not verifier-specific.

\paragraph{Capability gap hypothesis.}
We evaluate three generators spanning a 46-point SC range on ProofWriter ($n{=}600$), all paired with the identical DeBERTa-large-mnli verifier (55.00\% standalone accuracy). NLI combo gain decays monotonically as generator capability rises (Table~\ref{tab:cross_generator_nli}): Mistral-7B (SC${=}$39.33\%) gains $+10.67$\,pp at $w{=}5$ ($p{<}0.0001$), while Qwen2.5-14B (SC${=}$73.50\%, $+1.83$\,pp, $p{=}0.144$) and Qwen3-14B (SC${=}$85.00\%, $+1.83$\,pp, $p{=}0.052$) both plateau near zero gain. The three-point trend confirms the Capability Gap Hypothesis (Figure~\ref{fig:capability-gap}): meaningful NLI escape requires the verifier's standalone accuracy to approach or exceed the generator's SC baseline. When the generator substantially outperforms the verifier (gap $< -18$\,pp), the 12 SC traces overwhelm the NLI signal regardless of weight.

